      "message": "Nit / General comment.\n\nanother way of checking a sequence of call is to use inOrder.\n\nfor example this:\n```\nverify(foo).setActive(dev1);\nverify(foo, never()).setActive(dev2);\nverify(foo).setActive(dev1);\nverify(foo).setActive(dev2);\nverify(foo, times(2)).setActive(dev1);\n```\n\ncan be wrote:\n```\nmInOrder \u003d inOrder(foo);\n[...]\n\nmInOrder.verify(foo).setActive(dev1);\nmInOrder.verify(foo, never()).setActive(dev2);\nmInOrder.verify(foo).setActive(dev2);\nmInOrder.verify(foo).setActive(dev1);\n// because checked in order, we assure that this is coming after the setActive(dev2)\n// and therefor this is the second time since the begining of the test that \n// setActive(dev1) is called, but we don\u0027t have to specify it\n```\n\nThis bring the advantage that you not only checking what call was made but in which order.\nThis works best when we also check that there is no other call to the method at the end of the test with \n```\nmInOrder.verify(foo, never()).setActive(any());\n```",
